com/users/AThousandShips","html_url":"https://github.com/AThousandShips","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-07-10T21:19:11Z","updated_at":"2025-07-10T21:19:11Z","body":"I'd say that adding to the documentation to explain what local coordinates is would be excessive, I don't know of any other place that clarification is done, it's assumed users have read the documentation on how the coordinate systems work and understands them I'd say, they're linked in `Node2D`","author_association":"MEMBER","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3059138254/reactions","total_count":2,"+1":2,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3287625004","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/108447#issuecomment-3287625004","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/108447","id":3287625004,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc7D9S0s","user":{"login":"MadeScientist","id":190612698,"node_id":"U_kgDOC1yE2g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/190612698?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist","html_url":"https://github.com/MadeScientist","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MadeScientist/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-09-13T05:47:27Z","updated_at":"2025-09-13T05:49:19Z","body":"> I'd say that adding to the documentation to explain what local coordinates is would be excessive, I don't know of any other place that clarification is done, it's assumed users have read the documentation on how the coordinate systems work and understands them I'd say, they're linked in `Node2D`\n\nHi, AThousandShips, sorry to disturb you again. After some time, when I revisited my question today, I realized that I had a major confusion in the basic concept, and now I might have a true understanding of the coordinate system issue. Although it sounds silly, I would like to share my current understanding. If there are still any misunderstandings, please help me.\n\nThe reason why I misunderstood is that when I first started learning about 'position' and 'global_position', some teachers would translate the former into another expression, namely 'local_position'. In fact, the meaning expressed by 'local' is' relative ', which means that any transformation of node2d is relative to the parent node(this is easy to understand and is also explained in the documentation). However, the 'local coordinate system' referred here means relative to the node itself. These two 'local's have different meanings. So in fact, the local coordinate system is something that will never change, similar to the number 1 in mathematics. If we use the parent-child relationship as an analogy, it's like a mouse input event being as a child node of the current object, and its transformation is always default.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3287625004/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]